1. Arrangement of elements in the modern periodic table

Elements are arranged according to two main principles:

1. Increasing atomic number
(each element has one more proton than the previous one)


2. Aufbau principle
(electrons fill sublevels in order of increasing energy: 1s → 2s → 2p → 3s → 3p → 4s → 3d → 4p… etc.)




---

⭐ 2. Sublevel Arrangement and Blocks

The periodic table is divided into four blocks, depending on the last sublevel being filled:

1. s-block

Left side of the table

Groups 1 (IA) and 2 (IIA)

Last electron enters the s sublevel


2. p-block

Right side of the table

Groups 13–18

Last electron enters the p sublevel


3. d-block (Transition elements)

Middle of the table

Groups 3–12

Last electron enters the d sublevel


4. f-block (Inner transition elements)

Lanthanides and Actinides

Last electron enters the f sublevel



---

⭐ 3. Types of elements

Based on their electron configuration, elements fall into four categories:

1. Noble gases

Group 18

Stable electron configuration (ns² np⁶)

Very unreactive


2. Representative elements

s-block + p-block (except noble gases)

Show clear periodic trends


3. Main transition elements

d-block

Electrons added to the d sublevel


4. Inner transition elements

f-block

Electrons added to the f sublevel



---

⭐ 4. Atomic Radius

Definition:
Half the distance between the nuclei of two identical atoms in a diatomic molecule.

Measured in picometers (pm).


---

⭐ 5. Trends in Atomic Radius

A) Across a period (left → right)

Atomic radius decreases

Reason:

Nuclear charge increases (more protons)

Electrons are pulled closer to the nucleus

No increase in energy levels


Largest in a period: Group 1
Smallest: Group 17


---

B) Down a group (top → bottom)

Atomic radius increases

Reasons:

More energy levels

Increased shielding from inner electrons

Less attraction between nucleus and outer electrons



---

⭐ 6. Ionic Radius vs Atomic Radius

In Metals (forming positive ions):

Cation radius < Atomic radius

Because:

Metal loses electrons

Nuclear charge pulls remaining electrons closer

Radius becomes smaller


Example:

Na⁺ radius is smaller than Na atom


In Nonmetals (forming negative ions):

Anion radius > Atomic radius

Because:

Gaining electrons increases repulsion between electrons

Radius expands


Example:

Cl⁻ radius is larger than Cl atom



---

⭐ Example Table Explained

For ions like Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺:

Fe³⁺ is smaller than Fe²⁺
→ because it has more protons pulling fewer electrons, so stronger attraction = smaller radius.

